Now I see another issue: The LTB trigger in `collect_triggers` (lines 443-458) pushes ALL registered cards' LTB triggers, regardless of whether they have a `LeavesBattlefield` triggered ability. The `desc` might be empty if the card doesn't declare a `TriggerKind::LeavesBattlefield` in its `triggered_abilities`, but the trigger is still pushed!

Compare with the ETB-watch triggers (lines 367-392) which DO check `!desc.is_empty()`:


```rust
if !desc.is_empty() {
    let trigger = PendingTrigger::EnterWatch {...};
    ...
}
```

But the LTB triggers don't check for empty desc:
```rust
if registry.get(card_id).is_some() {
    let desc = trigger_description(registry, card_id, &crate::cards::TriggerKind::LeavesBattlefield, false);
    let trigger = PendingTrigger::LeftBattlefield {...};
    // LTB triggers go on AP side
    ap_triggers.push(trigger); // pushed regardless of desc being empty!
}
```

This means EVERY registered card that leaves the battlefield gets a trigger pushed, even if it has no actual LTB ability defined—the ETB logic guards against this with an empty check, but the LTB logic doesn't. The LTB trigger itself works correctly for Fiend Hunter—it's properly dispatched and resolved, calling `on_leave_battlefield` which correctly retrieves the exiled creature from the card state. The real issue is just that ETB trigger resolution fails when Fiend Hunter isn't on the battlefield. Let me dig deeper into the `on_leave_battlefield` implementation to see if there's an edge case I'm missing, like what happens if Fiend Hunter is destroyed while the exiled creature is also being removed.

Checking the zone validation logic...

Wait, I need to reconsider the trigger ordering issue. The `ap_triggers` are actually resolved first despite being pushed last, which means a Fiend Hunter controlled by the non-active player would have its LTB trigger resolve before NAP triggers from the same batch. This is incorrect—the LTB trigger should belong to the NAP and resolve in their trigger order. The real problem case is when FH's ETB trigger is still on the stack when it leaves the battlefield, so both triggers end up stacked together and need proper ordering to determine which resolves first. Looking at the `process_triggers` function, I can see it collects triggers, then resolves them in LIFO order while continuously collecting new ones. Since the LTB trigger resolves first and does nothing, then the ETB trigger checks if FH is still on the battlefield—which it isn't—the exile never happens. This confirms the core issue: the ETB trigger's condition fails because the source has already left the battlefield by the time it resolves.

The rest of the implementation checks out—mana costs, power/toughness, creature types, the "you may" optionality, the "another" restriction, token handling, and the controller reset all work correctly. The existing test suite covers the normal ETB case, the LTB return case, targeting your own creatures, and the choice presentation, and they all pass.

Now I'm writing up the audit report to the output file.

