## Audit — 2026-04-04 09:14

**Oracle text source**: Scryfall API (pre-fetched)
**Oracle text**: When this creature enters, you may exile another target creature. When this creature leaves the battlefield, return the exiled card to the battlefield under its owner's control.
**Type line**: Creature — Human Cleric
**Status**: ISSUE

### Summary

One engine-level bug affects Fiend Hunter's behavior in a specific but rules-significant scenario:

**ETB trigger dropped when source leaves before resolution** (`mtg-engine/src/triggers.rs` lines 893–899):

```rust
PendingTrigger::EnteredBattlefield { object_id, card_id, .. } => {
    // Verify the object is still on the battlefield.
    if state.get_object(object_id).map(|o| o.zone == Zone::Battlefield).unwrap_or(false) {
        if let Some(behavior) = registry.get(card_id) {
            behavior.on_enter_battlefield(state, object_id, registry);
        }
    }
}
```

The guard `o.zone == Zone::Battlefield` silently swallows the ETB trigger when Fiend Hunter has been killed or bounced in response to its own ETB trigger. Per the official ruling:

> *"If Fiend Hunter leaves the battlefield before its first ability has resolved, its second ability will trigger and do nothing. Then its first ability will resolve and exile the target creature indefinitely."*

The correct behavior is: the LTB trigger fires first (LIFO) and does nothing (no exile has occurred yet), then the ETB trigger resolves and exiles the creature permanently. Instead, the engine skips the ETB trigger entirely and no exile occurs at all.

All other aspects of the card check out: "you may" optionality, "another" exclusion, "under its owner's control" controller reset, LTB trigger dispatching and resolution, token non-return, and card data (cost, P/T, types, subtypes) are all implemented correctly.
